+\cl[inline]{Totality hypothesis and scope.
+The conjecture is proved below under \textbf{totality} only: $\delta_{in}$, $\lambda_{in}$, $\delta_{out}$ and $\lambda_{out}$ are total on the domains declared in~\cref{def:probDefTransducer}, which~\cref{def:consistency}'s partiality clause makes a hypothesis and not a consequence.
+Partial transducers, including the divergence witness above, are out of scope and are stated as such, not proved: for them the produced-trace language has to be chosen, and the choice decides whether the system may step outside a transducer's domain to end the trace there.
+Two further hypotheses are used and are the only reading of~\cref{def:probDef} the proof needs.
+\textbf{Termination:} the system chooses when the trace ends and traces are non-empty, as in the note after~\cref{def:probDef}.
+\textbf{Definability:} $\psiin$ and $\psiout$ are the languages $\Lin$ and $\Lout$ defined in the statement below, which are regular but not always $\text{LTL}_f$-definable; the proof uses only that they are those languages, so it holds verbatim for any regular objective in their place.}
+
+\begin{theorem}\label{thm:monolithic}
+Let $\Tin, \Tout$ be total transducers, and read~\cref{def:probDefTransducer} under the termination reading recorded in the note after~\cref{def:probDef}.
+For a finite trace $\pi = v_0 \cdots v_n$ let $q_{in,0}$ be the initial state and $q_{in,t+1} = \delta_{in}(q_{in,t}, v_t)$, and likewise $q_{out,t}$ from $q_{out,0}$ and $\delta_{out}$.
+Set
+\begin{align*}
+    \Lin &= \{\pi : \forall t \le n.\ v_t \cap \Iknown = \lambda_{in}(q_{in,t}, v_t \cap \Ifree)\}, \\
+    \Lout &= \{\pi : \forall t \le n.\ v_t \cap \Oknown = \lambda_{out}(q_{out,t}, v_t \cap (\mathcal{I} \cup \Ofree))\},
+\end{align*}
+and let $\psiin, \psiout$ be objectives with these languages.
+Then~\cref{def:probDefTransducer} for $(\varphi, \Tin, \Tout)$ has a solution if and only if the plain game $G$ with environment inputs $\mathcal{I}$, system outputs $\mathcal{O}$, the Mealy turn order and objective $\theta = \psiin \rightarrow (\varphi \wedge \psiout)$ is realizable.
+Moreover, a finite-state solution yields a finite-state winning strategy of $G$, and a realizable $G$ has a finite-state winning strategy that projects to a finite-state solution.
+\end{theorem}
+
+\begin{proof}
+A \emph{play} is an infinite sequence $u = u_0 u_1 \cdots$ of letters of $2^{\mathcal{I} \cup \mathcal{O}}$, and it \emph{satisfies} an objective if some non-empty finite prefix does.
+A plain strategy is $\sigma : (2^{\mathcal{I} \cup \mathcal{O}})^* \times 2^{\mathcal{I}} \to 2^{\mathcal{O}}$, and its play against inputs $(i_t)$ has $u_t = i_t \cup \sigma(u_0 \cdots u_{t-1}, i_t)$.
+By the definition of agreement, $\pi \in \Lin$ if and only if $\pi$ agrees with $\Sin$ at every step, and $\pi \in \Lout$ if and only if it agrees with $\Sout$.
+Both languages are prefix-closed.
+
+\textbf{Uniqueness.}
+For a strategy $S_C$ and an $\Ifree$-sequence $(f_t)$, for every $n$ there is exactly one trace of length $n+1$ that agrees with $\Sin, \Sout, S_C$, and these traces are the prefixes of one play, the \emph{agreeing play}.
+By induction on $t$: given $v_0 \cdots v_{t-1}$, the states $q_{in,t}, q_{out,t}$ exist by totality of $\delta_{in}, \delta_{out}$.
+Agreement forces $v_t \cap \Ifree = f_t$, then $v_t \cap \Iknown = \lambda_{in}(q_{in,t}, f_t)$, then $v_t \cap \Ofree = S_C(v_0 \cdots v_{t-1}, v_t \cap \mathcal{I})$, then $v_t \cap \Oknown = \lambda_{out}(q_{out,t}, v_t \cap (\mathcal{I} \cup \Ofree))$.
+Each is a value of a total function, so $v_t$ exists and is unique.
+This is the canonical turn order of~\cref{definition}, and the only place the Mealy order is used.
+
+\textbf{Direction $(\Rightarrow)$.}
+Let $T_C$ solve the problem, with strategy $S_C$, and define
+\[
+    \sigma(h, i) = S_C(h, i) \cup \lambda_{out}\big(q^{out}(h),\, i \cup S_C(h, i)\big),
+\]
+where $q^{out}(h)$ is the $\Tout$ state after $h$.
+Where $S_C(h, i)$ is undefined, which is only on $h$ outside $\Lin$, pick any value, since $\theta$ is vacuous there.
+If $T_C$ is finite-state, so is $\sigma$: it runs as the product of $T_C$ and $\Tout$.
+Let $u$ be the play of $\sigma$ against any input sequence.
+By construction every step of $u$ agrees with $\Tout$ and with $S_C$ on $\Ofree$, so every prefix of $u$ is in $\Lout$.
+Take the first $t$ with $u_t \cap \Iknown \neq \lambda_{in}(q_{in,t}, u_t \cap \Ifree)$, if any.
+If such a $t$ exists, then $u_0 \cdots u_t \notin \Lin$, so it satisfies $\theta$ vacuously.
+Otherwise every step of $u$ agrees with $\Tin$, so $u$ is the agreeing play for $(u_t \cap \Ifree)$.
+Since $S_C$ solves the problem some prefix $\pi$ satisfies $\varphi$, and all prefixes lie in $\Lin \cap \Lout$, so $\pi \models \psiin \wedge \varphi \wedge \psiout$ and hence $\pi \models \theta$.
+So $\sigma$ wins.
+
+\textbf{Direction $(\Leftarrow)$.}
+Let $\sigma$ win $G$.
+Since $\theta$ is regular, the product of $G$ with the DFA for $\theta$ is a reachability game with positional winning strategies, so $\sigma$ can be taken finite-state (finite-memory on $G$).
+Define $S_C(h, i) = \sigma(h, i) \cap \Ofree$, which is finite-state as well.
+Fix an $\Ifree$-sequence $(f_t)$ and let $w$ be the agreeing play.
+Build the play $u$ against $\sigma$ adaptively: $u_t \cap \Ifree = f_t$, $u_t \cap \Iknown = \lambda_{in}(q_{in,t}, f_t)$ with $q_{in,t}$ computed along $u$ itself, and $u_t \cap \mathcal{O} = \sigma(u_0 \cdots u_{t-1}, u_t \cap \mathcal{I})$.
+This uses totality of $\delta_{in}$ and $\lambda_{in}$.
+The adaptation is needed because $\sigma$ may deviate in $\Oknown$ so that $\delta_{in}$ leaves the states $w$ passes through, so a fixed copy of $w$'s inputs would not do.
+Every prefix of $u$ is in $\Lin$, so $\psiin$ holds at every prefix, and since $\sigma$ wins some prefix $\pi = u_0 \cdots u_n$ satisfies $\varphi \wedge \psiout$.
+Now $\pi \in \Lout$ gives agreement with $\Tout$, $\pi \in \Lin$ gives agreement with $\Tin$, and $u_t \cap \Ofree = S_C(u_0 \cdots u_{t-1}, u_t \cap \mathcal{I})$ by definition.
+So $\pi$ is a trace of length $n+1$ agreeing with $\Sin, \Sout, S_C$, and by uniqueness $w_0 \cdots w_n = \pi$.
+Hence $w$ has a prefix satisfying $\varphi$, for every $(f_t)$, so $T_C$ solves the problem.
+\end{proof}
+\cl[inline]{This settles the conjecture's ``to be proven'' under the hypotheses of the note above.
+The asymmetry shows in the proof: $\Tin$ constrains an environment-chosen letter, so a violation hands the system a win (the vacuous branch of $(\Rightarrow)$ and the adaptive adversary of $(\Leftarrow)$), and it is an assumption; $\Tout$ constrains a system-chosen letter, so a violation hands the system nothing, because $\varphi \wedge \psiout$ fails on the violating prefix, and it is a guarantee.
+Totality is used in the uniqueness step, in the definition of $\sigma$, and in the adaptive environment of $(\Leftarrow)$; without it $\Lin$ stops being the set of traces on which every step agrees, since a step at a $\delta$-dead state has no agreeing letter, which is the divergence witness above and is not argued here.}
